%U A400017 21,7,1,1,3,3,3,3,1,1,3,3,7,21,7,3,3,1,1,7,21,7
%N A400017 Irregular triangle read by rows: T(n,k) = A399964(A046072(n),k), 1 <= k <= A046072(n); for n >= 3, number of prime races modulo n of index 2^k.
%C A400017 Let n >= 3. A prime race modulo n of index 2^k is a pair of (H,S), where H is a proper subgroup of (Z/nZ)* of index 2^k which contains (Z/nZ)*^2, and S is a nontrivial coset of H. Equivalently, a prime race modulo n of index 2^k is a nontrivial coset T of a proper subgroup T_0 of Hom((Z/nZ)*,{+-1}) of order 2^(k-1). The correspondence between the two definitions is
%C A400017   T |-> (H,S) = ({x in (Z/nZ)* : chi(x) = 1 for all chi in T}, {x in (Z/nZ)* : chi(x) = -1 for all chi in T});
%C A400017   (H,S) |-> T = {chi in Hom((Z/nZ)*,{+-1}) : chi(x) = 1 for all x in H, chi(x) = -1 for all x in S}.
%C A400017 Here (Z/nZ)* is the multiplicative group of integers modulo n and (Z/nZ)*^2 is the subgroup of squares.
%C A400017 Since (Z/nZ)*/(Z/nZ)*^2 can been seen as a vector space over F_2 of dimension A046072(n), for a prime race (H,S) of index 2^k, H U S is a subspace of dimension n-k+1, and H is a subspace of dimension n-k in H U S. Equivalently, Hom((Z/nZ)*,{+-1}) is the F_2-dual of (Z/nZ)*/(Z/nZ)*^2; for a prime race T of index 2^k, T0 U T is a subspace of dimension k, in which T0 is a subspace of dimension k-1.
%C A400017 Assuming the generalized Riemann hypothesis (GRH) and the Grand Simplicity Hypothesis (GSH; called LI in page 29 of Greg Martin's 2010 link), then pi_{N;S}(x) > pi_{N;H}(x) occurs more often than not: the set {pi_{N;S}(x) > pi_{N;H}(x)} has logarithmic density greater than 1/2. This phenomenon is called "Chebyshev's bias".
%H A400017 Jianing Song, <a href="/A400017/b400017.txt">Table of n, a(n) for n = 1..10000</a> (rows n = 1..4524)
%H A400017 Greg Martin and Daniel Fiorilli, <a href="https://personal.math.ubc.ca/~gerg/slides/Chennai-31Aug10.pdf">Prime number races: An asymptotic formula for the densities</a>, Analytic and Combinatorial Number Theory, Institute of Mathematical Sciences, Chennai, India. August 31, 2010.
%H A400017 Wikipedia, <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chebyshev%27s_bias">Chebyshev's bias</a>.
%F A400017 Let m = A046072(n), then T(n,k) = A022166(m,k)*(2^k - 1), 1 <= k <= m.
%F A400017 T(n,1) = 2^m - 1: A prime race T modulo n of index 2 is just a nonprincipal real Dirichlet character modulo n, and there are 2^m - 1.
%F A400017 T(n,2) = (2^m - 1)*(2^(m-1) - 1): A prime race T modulo n of index 4 is just a pair of distinct nonprincipal real Dirichlet characters modulo n, and so the number of binomial(2^m - 1, 2).
%F A400017 T(n,m-1) = (2^m - 1)*(2^(m-1) - 1): For a prime race (H,S) modulo n of index 2^(m-1), the coset S is the union of two distinct cosets S1, S2 of (Z/nZ)*^2 in (Z/nZ)*, and H = (Z/nZ)*^2 U (S1*S2).
%F A400017 T(n,m) = 2^m - 1: For a prime race (H,S) modulo n of index 2^m, we have H = (Z/nZ)*^2 and S is one of the 2^m - 1 cosets of (Z/nZ)*^2 in (Z/nZ)*.
